  "prediction": "**Leopard Attacks and Kills Toddler in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda**\n\nA devastating incident has occurred in Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda, where a leopard attacked and killed a toddler. The 2-year-old child, Elisha Nabugyere, was left in the care of a nanny at the staff quarters of Mweya Safari Lodge when the leopard snatched him on Friday night. The nanny heard the child scream for help but was unable to intervene as the leopard dragged the boy into the bushes.\n\nThe child's mother, a game ranger at the park, was working at the time of the incident. The father, SSP Francis Manana Nabugyere, an officer at the police senior Command and staff college Bwebajja, expressed his shock and grief over the loss of his son. He has called for heightened security measures to protect staff and children at the park.\n\nThe Uganda Wildlife Authority (UWA) has launched a hunt to capture the leopard, which is considered a threat to human life after consuming human flesh. According to UWA communications manager Bashir Hangi, efforts are underway to track down the leopard and evaluate options such as relocating it to a different area.\n\nThis incident is the fourth unfortunate event to occur at the UWA in recent weeks. In April, a pride of 11 lions died in Queen Elizabeth National Park, a French tourist died on park grounds, and another lion died at the end of the month.\n\nThe UWA has offered a coffin to the family of the deceased child, but the father has expressed his expectation for reasonable compensation for the loss of his son. The incident has sent shockwaves throughout Uganda, where leopard attacks on humans are rare.\n\n**Key Details:**\n\n* Date of incident: Friday night\n* Location: Mweya Safari Lodge, Queen Elizabeth National Park, Uganda\n* Victim: 2-year-old Elisha Nabugyere\n* Perpetrator: Leopard\n* Status of leopard: Hunt is underway to capture and relocate the leopard\n* UWA response: Offered a coffin to the family, launched a hunt to capture the leopard\n* Compensation: Father expects reasonable compensation for the loss of his son",
